    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a fair theoretical point, but I'm looking for a more practical, data-driven assessment of the risks. Even if we assume the cognitive capacity is there, a curriculum of this intensity demands immediate, seamless fluency in formal syntax to grasp high-level concepts without delay. If a student has to constantly \"translate\" or navigate the gap between their natural vernacular and the required academic register, aren't we essentially looking at a deficit in the executive function or the cognitive rigor needed for this specific environment? In a competitive setting, isn't it more accurate to view a reliance on non-standard linguistics as a marker of lower academic readiness for the demands of a high-tier institution?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
